.SS xonsh shell
.sp
Jedi is a preinstalled extension in xonsh shell \%<https://\:xon\:.sh/\:contents\:.html>\&.
Run the following command to enable:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
xontrib load jedi
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and many more!
.SS Tab Completion in the Python Shell
.sp
Jedi is a dependency of IPython. Autocompletion in IPython is therefore
possible without additional configuration.
.sp
Here is an example video \%<https://\:vimeo\:.com/\:122332037> how REPL completion
can look like in a different shell.
.sp
There are two different options how you can use Jedi autocompletion in
your \fBpython\fP interpreter. One with your custom \fB$HOME/.pythonrc.py\fP file
and one that uses \fBPYTHONSTARTUP\fP\&.
.SS Using \fBPYTHONSTARTUP\fP
.sp
To use Jedi completion in Python interpreter, add the following in your shell
setup (e.g., \fB\&.bashrc\fP). This works only on Linux/Mac, because readline is
not available on Windows. If you still want Jedi autocompletion in your REPL,
just use IPython instead:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
export PYTHONSTARTUP=\(dq$(python \-m jedi repl)\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then you will be able to use Jedi completer in your Python interpreter:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ python
Python 3.14.0+ (default, Jul 20 2020, 22:15:08)
[GCC 4.6.1] on linux2
Type \(dqhelp\(dq, \(dqcopyright\(dq, \(dqcredits\(dq or \(dqlicense\(dq for more information.
>>> import os
>>> os.path.join(\(aqa\(aq, \(aqb\(aq).split().in<TAB>            # doctest: +SKIP
\&..dex   ..sert
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Using a Custom \fB$HOME/.pythonrc.py\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.utils.setup_readline(namespace_module=<module \(aq__main__\(aq from \(aq/usr/bin/sphinx\-build\(aq>, fuzzy=False)
This function sets up \fBreadline\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:readline\:.html#\:module-readline> to use Jedi in a Python interactive
shell.
.sp
If you want to use a custom \fBPYTHONSTARTUP\fP file (typically
\fB$HOME/.pythonrc.py\fP), you can add this piece of code:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
try:
    from jedi.utils import setup_readline
except ImportError:
    # Fallback to the stdlib readline completer if it is installed.
    # Taken from http://docs.python.org/2/library/rlcompleter.html
    print(\(dqJedi is not installed, falling back to readline\(dq)
    try:
        import readline
        import rlcompleter
        readline.parse_and_bind(\(dqtab: complete\(dq)
    except ImportError:
        print(\(dqReadline is not installed either. No tab completion is enabled.\(dq)
else:
    setup_readline()
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will fallback to the readline completer if Jedi is not installed.
The readline completer will only complete names in the global namespace,
so for example:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ran<TAB>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
will complete to \fBrange\fP\&.
.sp
With Jedi the following code:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
range(10).cou<TAB>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
will complete to \fBrange(10).count\fP, this does not work with the default
cPython \fBreadline\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:readline\:.html#\:module-readline> completer.
.sp
You will also need to add \fBexport PYTHONSTARTUP=$HOME/.pythonrc.py\fP to
your shell profile (usually \fB\&.bash_profile\fP or \fB\&.profile\fP if you use
bash).
.UNINDENT
.SS Recipes
.sp
Here are some tips on how to use Jedi efficiently.
.SS Type Hinting
.sp
If Jedi cannot detect the type of a function argument correctly (due to the
dynamic nature of Python), you can help it by hinting the type using
one of the docstring/annotation styles below. \fBOnly gradual typing will
always work\fP, all the docstring solutions are glorified hacks and more
complicated cases will probably not work.
.SS Official Gradual Typing (Recommended)
.sp
You can read a lot about Python\(aqs gradual typing system in the corresponding
PEPs like:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 484 \%<https://\:www\:.python\:.org/\:dev/\:peps/\:pep-0484> as an introduction
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 526 \%<https://\:www\:.python\:.org/\:dev/\:peps/\:pep-0526> for variable annotations
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 589 \%<https://\:www\:.python\:.org/\:dev/\:peps/\:pep-0589> for \fBTypeDict\fP
.IP \(bu 2
There are probably more :)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Below you can find a few examples how you can use this feature.
.sp
Function annotations:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def myfunction(node: ProgramNode, foo: str) \-> None:
    \(dq\(dq\(dqDo something with a \(ga\(ganode\(ga\(ga.

    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    node.| # complete here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assignment, for\-loop and with\-statement type hints:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import typing
x: int = foo()
y: typing.Optional[int] = 3

key: str
value: Employee
for key, value in foo.items():
    pass

f: Union[int, float]
with foo() as f:
    print(f + 3)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
PEP\-0484 should be supported in its entirety. Feel free to open issues if that
is not the case. You can also use stub files.
.SS Sphinx style
.sp
\%<http://\:www\:.sphinx-doc\:.org/\:en/\:stable/\:domains\:.html#\:info-field-lists>
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def myfunction(node, foo):
    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    Do something with a \(ga\(ganode\(ga\(ga.

    :type node: ProgramNode
    :param str foo: foo parameter description
    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    node.| # complete here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Epydoc
.sp
\%<http://\:epydoc\:.sourceforge\:.net/\:manual-fields\:.html>
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def myfunction(node):
    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    Do something with a \(ga\(ganode\(ga\(ga.

    @type node: ProgramNode
    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    node.| # complete here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Numpydoc
.sp
\%<https://\:github\:.com/\:numpy/\:numpy/\:blob/\:master/\:doc/\:HOWTO_DOCUMENT\:.rst\:.txt>
.sp
In order to support the numpydoc format, you need to install the numpydoc \%<https://\:pypi\:.python\:.org/\:pypi/\:numpydoc> package.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def foo(var1, var2, long_var_name=\(aqhi\(aq):
    r\(dq\(dq\(dq
    A one\-line summary that does not use variable names or the
    function name.

    ...

    Parameters
    \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
    var1 : array_like
        Array_like means all those objects \-\- lists, nested lists,
        etc. \-\- that can be converted to an array. We can also
        refer to variables like \(gavar1\(ga.
    var2 : int
        The type above can either refer to an actual Python type
        (e.g. \(ga\(gaint\(ga\(ga), or describe the type of the variable in more
        detail, e.g. \(ga\(ga(N,) ndarray\(ga\(ga or \(ga\(gaarray_like\(ga\(ga.
    long_variable_name : {\(aqhi\(aq, \(aqho\(aq}, optional
        Choices in brackets, default first when optional.

    ...

    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    var2.| # complete here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Features and Limitations
.sp
Jedi\(aqs main API calls and features are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Autocompletion: \fBScript.complete()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.complete>; It\(aqs also possible to get it
working in your REPL (IPython, etc.) \%<#\:repl-completion>
.IP \(bu 2
Goto/Type Inference: \fBScript.goto()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.goto> and \fBScript.infer()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.infer>
.IP \(bu 2
Static Analysis: \fBScript.get_names()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.get_names> and \fBScript.get_syntax_errors()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.get_syntax_errors>
.IP \(bu 2
Refactorings: \fBScript.rename()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.rename>, \fBScript.inline()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.inline>,
\fBScript.extract_variable()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.extract_variable> and \fBScript.extract_function()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.extract_function>
.IP \(bu 2
Code Search: \fBScript.search()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.search> and \fBProject.search()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Project\:.search>
.UNINDENT
.SS Basic Features
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Python 3.10+ support
.IP \(bu 2
Ignores syntax errors and wrong indentation
.IP \(bu 2
Can deal with complex module / function / class structures
.IP \(bu 2
Great \fBvirtualenv\fP/\fBvenv\fP support
.IP \(bu 2
Works great with Python\(aqs type hinting \%<#\:type-hinting>,
.IP \(bu 2
Understands stub files
.IP \(bu 2
Can infer function arguments for sphinx, epydoc and basic numpydoc docstrings
.IP \(bu 2
Is overall a very solid piece of software that has been refined for a long
time. Bug reports are very welcome and are usually fixed within a few weeks.
.UNINDENT
.SS Supported Python Features
.sp
Jedi supports many of the widely used Python features:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
builtins
.IP \(bu 2
returns, yields, yield from
.IP \(bu 2
tuple assignments / array indexing / dictionary indexing / star unpacking
.IP \(bu 2
with\-statement / exception handling
.IP \(bu 2
\fB*args\fP / \fB**kwargs\fP
.IP \(bu 2
decorators / lambdas / closures
.IP \(bu 2
generators / iterators
.IP \(bu 2
descriptors: property / staticmethod / classmethod / custom descriptors
.IP \(bu 2
some magic methods: \fB__call__\fP, \fB__iter__\fP, \fB__next__\fP, \fB__get__\fP,
\fB__getitem__\fP, \fB__init__\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist.append()\fP, \fBset.add()\fP, \fBlist.extend()\fP, etc.
.IP \(bu 2
(nested) list comprehensions / ternary expressions
.IP \(bu 2
relative imports
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgetattr()\fP / \fB__getattr__\fP / \fB__getattribute__\fP
.IP \(bu 2
function annotations
.IP \(bu 2
simple/typical \fBsys.path\fP modifications
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisinstance\fP checks for if/while/assert
.IP \(bu 2
namespace packages (includes \fBpkgutil\fP, \fBpkg_resources\fP and PEP420 namespaces)
.IP \(bu 2
Django / Flask / Buildout support
.IP \(bu 2
Understands Pytest fixtures
.UNINDENT
.SS Limitations
.sp
In general Jedi\(aqs limit is quite high, but for very big projects or very
complex code, sometimes Jedi intentionally stops type inference, to avoid
hanging for a long time.
.sp
Additionally there are some Python patterns Jedi does not support. This is
intentional and below should be a complete list:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Arbitrary metaclasses: Some metaclasses like enums and dataclasses are
reimplemented in Jedi to make them work. Most of the time stubs are good
enough to get type inference working, even when metaclasses are involved.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsetattr()\fP, \fB__import__()\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Writing to some dicts: \fBglobals()\fP, \fBlocals()\fP, \fBobject.__dict__\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Manipulations of instances outside the instance variables without using
methods
.UNINDENT
.SS Performance Issues
.sp
Importing \fBnumpy\fP can be quite slow sometimes, as well as loading the
builtins the first time. If you want to speed things up, you could preload
libraries in Jedi, with \fBpreload_module()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.preload_module>\&. However, once loaded, this
should not be a problem anymore.  The same is true for huge modules like
\fBPySide\fP, \fBwx\fP, \fBtensorflow\fP, \fBpandas\fP, etc.
.sp
Jedi does not have a very good cache layer. This is probably the biggest and
only architectural issue \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi/\:issues/\:1059> in
Jedi. Unfortunately it is not easy to change that. Dave Halter is thinking
about rewriting Jedi in Rust, but it has taken Jedi more than 8 years to reach
version 1.0, a rewrite will probably also take years.
.SS Security
.SS For \fBScript\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script>
.sp
Security is an important topic for Jedi\&. By default, no code is executed
within Jedi. As long as you write pure Python, everything is inferred
statically. If you enable \fBload_unsafe_extensions=True\fP for your
\fBProject\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Project> and you use builtin modules (\fBc_builtin\fP) Jedi will execute
those modules. If you don\(aqt trust a code base, please do not enable that
option. It might lead to arbitrary code execution.
.SS For \fBInterpreter\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Interpreter>
.sp
If you want security for \fBInterpreter\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Interpreter>, \fBdo not\fP use it. Jedi does
execute properties and in general is not very careful to avoid code execution.
This is intentional: Most people trust the code bases they have imported,
because at that point a malicious code base would have had code execution
already.
.SS API Overview
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This documentation is mostly for Plugin developers, who want to
improve their editors/IDE with Jedi.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The API consists of a few different parts:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The main starting points for complete/goto: \fBScript\fP and
\fBInterpreter\fP\&. If you work with Jedi you want to understand these
classes first.
.IP \(bu 2
API Result Classes \%<#\:api-classes>
.IP \(bu 2
Python Versions/Virtualenv Support with functions like
\fBfind_system_environments()\fP and \fBfind_virtualenvs()\fP
.IP \(bu 2
A way to work with different Folders / Projects
.IP \(bu 2
Helpful functions: \fBpreload_module()\fP and \fBset_debug_function()\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
The methods that you are most likely going to use to work with Jedi are the
following ones:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
\fBScript.complete\fP
T}	T{
Completes objects under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.goto\fP
T}	T{
Goes to the name that defined the object under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.infer\fP
T}	T{
Return the definitions of under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.help\fP
T}	T{
Used to display a help window to users.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.get_signatures\fP
T}	T{
Return the function object of the call under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.get_references\fP
T}	T{
Lists all references of a variable in a project.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.get_context\fP
T}	T{
Returns the scope context under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.get_names\fP
T}	T{
Returns names defined in the current file.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.get_syntax_errors\fP
T}	T{
Lists all syntax errors in the current file.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.rename\fP
T}	T{
Renames all references of the variable under the cursor.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.inline\fP
T}	T{
Inlines a variable under the cursor. This is basically the opposite of extracting a variable. For example with the cursor on bar::.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.extract_variable\fP
T}	T{
Moves an expression to a new statement.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.extract_function\fP
T}	T{
Moves an expression to a new function.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.search\fP
T}	T{
Searches a name in the current file.
T}
_
T{
\fBScript.complete_search\fP
T}	T{
Like \fBScript.search()\fP, but completes that string.
T}
_
T{
\fBProject.search\fP
T}	T{
Searches a name in the whole project.
T}
_
T{
\fBProject.complete_search\fP
T}	T{
Like \fBScript.search()\fP, but completes that string.
T}
.TE
.SS Script
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.Script(code=None, *, path=None, environment=None, project=None)
A Script is the base for completions, goto or whatever you want to do with
Jedi. The counter part of this class is \fBInterpreter\fP, which works
with actual dictionaries and can work with a REPL. This class
should be used when a user edits code in an editor.
.sp
You can either use the \fBcode\fP parameter or \fBpath\fP to read a file.
Usually you\(aqre going to want to use both of them (in an editor).
.sp
The Script\(aqs \fBsys.path\fP is very customizable:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
If \fIproject\fP is provided with a \fBsys_path\fP, that is going to be used.
.IP \(bu 2
If \fIenvironment\fP is provided, its \fBsys.path\fP will be used
(see \fBEnvironment.get_sys_path\fP);
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise \fBsys.path\fP will match that of the default environment of
Jedi, which typically matches the sys path that was used at the time
when Jedi was imported.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Most methods have a \fBline\fP and a \fBcolumn\fP parameter. Lines in Jedi are
always 1\-based and columns are always zero based. To avoid repetition they
are not always documented. You can omit both line and column. Jedi will
then just do whatever action you are calling at the end of the file. If you
provide only the line, just will complete at the end of that line.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
By default \fBjedi.settings.fast_parser\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.settings\:.fast_parser> is enabled, which means
that parso reuses modules (i.e. they are not immutable). With this setting
Jedi is \fBnot thread safe\fP and it is also not safe to use multiple
\fBScript\fP instances and its definitions at the same time.
.sp
If you are a normal plugin developer this should not be an issue. It is
an issue for people that do more complex stuff with Jedi.
.sp
This is purely a performance optimization and works pretty well for all
typical usages, however consider to turn the setting off if it causes
you problems. See also
this discussion \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi/\:issues/\:1240>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcode\fP (\fIstr\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>) \-\- The source code of the current file, separated by newlines.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath\fP (\fIstr\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>\fI or \fP\fIpathlib.Path\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:pathlib\:.html#\:pathlib\:.Path>\fI or \fP\fINone\fP) \-\- The path of the file in the file system, or \fB\(aq\(aq\fP if
it hasn\(aqt been saved yet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenvironment\fP (\fIEnvironment\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.environment\:.Environment>) \-\- Provide a predefined Environment
to work with a specific Python version or virtualenv.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBproject\fP (\fIProject\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Project>) \-\- Provide a \fBProject\fP to make sure finding
references works well, because the right folder is searched. There are
also ways to modify the sys path and other things.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B complete(line=None, column=None, *, fuzzy=False)
Completes objects under the cursor.
.sp
Those objects contain information about the completions, more than just
names.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBfuzzy\fP \-\- Default False. Will return fuzzy completions, which means
that e.g. \fBooa\fP will match \fBfoobar\fP\&.
.TP
.B Returns
Completion objects, sorted by name. Normal names appear
before \(dqprivate\(dq names that start with \fB_\fP and those appear
before magic methods and name mangled names that start with \fB__\fP\&.
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBCompletion\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Completion>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B infer(line=None, column=None, *, only_stubs=False, prefer_stubs=False)
Return the definitions of under the cursor. It is basically a wrapper
around Jedi\(aqs type inference.
.sp
This method follows complicated paths and returns the end, not the
first definition. The big difference between \fBgoto()\fP and
\fBinfer()\fP is that \fBgoto()\fP doesn\(aqt
follow imports and statements. Multiple objects may be returned,
because depending on an option you can have two different versions of a
function.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBonly_stubs\fP \-\- Only return stubs for this method.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprefer_stubs\fP \-\- Prefer stubs to Python objects for this method.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B goto(line=None, column=None, *, follow_imports=False, follow_builtin_imports=False, only_stubs=False, prefer_stubs=False)
Goes to the name that defined the object under the cursor. Optionally
you can follow imports.
Multiple objects may be returned, depending on an if you can have two
different versions of a function.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfollow_imports\fP \-\- The method will follow imports.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfollow_builtin_imports\fP \-\- If \fBfollow_imports\fP is True will try
to look up names in builtins (i.e. compiled or extension modules).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBonly_stubs\fP \-\- Only return stubs for this method.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprefer_stubs\fP \-\- Prefer stubs to Python objects for this method.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B search(string, *, all_scopes=False)
Searches a name in the current file. For a description of how the
search string should look like, please have a look at
\fBProject.search()\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBall_scopes\fP (\fIbool\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:bool>) \-\- Default False; searches not only for
definitions on the top level of a module level, but also in
functions and classes.
.TP
.B Yields
\fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B complete_search(string, **kwargs)
Like \fBScript.search()\fP, but completes that string. If you want to
have all possible definitions in a file you can also provide an empty
string.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBall_scopes\fP (\fIbool\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:bool>) \-\- Default False; searches not only for
definitions on the top level of a module level, but also in
functions and classes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfuzzy\fP \-\- Default False. Will return fuzzy completions, which means
that e.g. \fBooa\fP will match \fBfoobar\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Yields
\fBCompletion\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Completion>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B help(line=None, column=None)
Used to display a help window to users.  Uses \fBScript.goto()\fP and
returns additional definitions for keywords and operators.
.sp
Typically you will want to display \fBBaseName.docstring()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.BaseName\:.docstring> to the
user for all the returned definitions.
.sp
The additional definitions are \fBName(...).type == \(aqkeyword\(aq\fP\&.
These definitions do not have a lot of value apart from their docstring
attribute, which contains the output of Python\(aqs \fBhelp()\fP function.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_references(line=None, column=None, **kwargs)
Lists all references of a variable in a project. Since this can be
quite hard to do for Jedi, if it is too complicated, Jedi will stop
searching.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinclude_builtins\fP \-\- Default \fBTrue\fP\&. If \fBFalse\fP, checks if a definition
is a builtin (e.g. \fBsys\fP) and in that case does not return it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscope\fP \-\- Default \fB\(aqproject\(aq\fP\&. If \fB\(aqfile\(aq\fP, include references in
the current module only.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_signatures(line=None, column=None)
Return the function object of the call under the cursor.
.sp
E.g. if the cursor is here:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
abs(# <\-\- cursor is here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This would return the \fBabs\fP function. On the other hand:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
abs()# <\-\- cursor is here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This would return an empty list..
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBSignature\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Signature>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_context(line=None, column=None)
Returns the scope context under the cursor. This basically means the
function, class or module where the cursor is at.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
\fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_names(**kwargs)
Returns names defined in the current file.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBall_scopes\fP \-\- If True lists the names of all scopes instead of
only the module namespace.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefinitions\fP \-\- If True lists the names that have been defined by a
class, function or a statement (\fBa = b\fP returns \fBa\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreferences\fP \-\- If True lists all the names that are not listed by
\fBdefinitions=True\fP\&. E.g. \fBa = b\fP returns \fBb\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_syntax_errors()
Lists all syntax errors in the current file.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBSyntaxError\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.errors\:.SyntaxError>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B rename(line=None, column=None, *, new_name)
Renames all references of the variable under the cursor.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBnew_name\fP \-\- The variable under the cursor will be renamed to this
string.
.TP
.B Raises
\fBRefactoringError\fP
.TP
.B Return type
\fBRefactoring\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.refactoring\:.Refactoring>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B extract_variable(line, column, *, new_name, until_line=None, until_column=None)
Moves an expression to a new statement.
.sp
For example if you have the cursor on \fBfoo\fP and provide a
\fBnew_name\fP called \fBbar\fP:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
foo = 3.1
x = int(foo + 1)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
the code above will become:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
foo = 3.1
bar = foo + 1
x = int(bar)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnew_name\fP \-\- The expression under the cursor will be renamed to
this string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuntil_line\fP (\fIint\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>) \-\- The the selection range ends at this line, when
omitted, Jedi will be clever and try to define the range itself.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuntil_column\fP (\fIint\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>) \-\- The the selection range ends at this column, when
omitted, Jedi will be clever and try to define the range itself.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Raises
\fBRefactoringError\fP
.TP
.B Return type
\fBRefactoring\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.refactoring\:.Refactoring>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B extract_function(line, column, *, new_name, until_line=None, until_column=None)
Moves an expression to a new function.
.sp
For example if you have the cursor on \fBfoo\fP and provide a
\fBnew_name\fP called \fBbar\fP:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
global_var = 3

def x():
    foo = 3.1
    x = int(foo + 1 + global_var)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
the code above will become:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
global_var = 3

def bar(foo):
    return int(foo + 1 + global_var)

def x():
    foo = 3.1
    x = bar(foo)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnew_name\fP \-\- The expression under the cursor will be replaced with
a function with this name.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuntil_line\fP (\fIint\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>) \-\- The the selection range ends at this line, when
omitted, Jedi will be clever and try to define the range itself.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuntil_column\fP (\fIint\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>) \-\- The the selection range ends at this column, when
omitted, Jedi will be clever and try to define the range itself.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Raises
\fBRefactoringError\fP
.TP
.B Return type
\fBRefactoring\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.refactoring\:.Refactoring>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B inline(line=None, column=None)
Inlines a variable under the cursor. This is basically the opposite of
extracting a variable. For example with the cursor on bar:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
foo = 3.1
bar = foo + 1
x = int(bar)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
the code above will become:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
foo = 3.1
x = int(foo + 1)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Raises
\fBRefactoringError\fP
.TP
.B Return type
\fBRefactoring\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.refactoring\:.Refactoring>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Interpreter
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.Interpreter(code, namespaces, *, project=None, **kwds)
Jedi\(aqs API for Python REPLs.
.sp
Implements all of the methods that are present in \fBScript\fP as well.
.sp
In addition to completions that normal REPL completion does like
\fBstr.upper\fP, Jedi also supports code completion based on static code
analysis. For example Jedi will complete \fBstr().upper\fP\&.
.sp
.EX
>>> from os.path import join
>>> namespace = locals()
>>> script = Interpreter(\(aqjoin(\(dq\(dq).up\(aq, [namespace])
>>> print(script.complete()[0].name)
upper
.EE
.sp
All keyword arguments are same as the arguments for \fBScript\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcode\fP (\fIstr\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>) \-\- Code to parse.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnamespaces\fP (\fIList\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:typing\:.html#\:typing\:.List>\fI[\fP\fIdict\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:dict>\fI]\fP) \-\- A list of namespace dictionaries such as the one
returned by \fBglobals()\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:globals> and \fBlocals()\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:locals>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Projects
.sp
Projects are a way to handle Python projects within Jedi. For simpler plugins
you might not want to deal with projects, but if you want to give the user more
flexibility to define sys paths and Python interpreters for a project,
\fBProject\fP is the perfect way to allow for that.
.sp
Projects can be saved to disk and loaded again, to allow project definitions to
be used across repositories.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.get_default_project(path=None)
If a project is not defined by the user, Jedi tries to define a project by
itself as well as possible. Jedi traverses folders until it finds one of
the following:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP 1. 3
A \fB\&.jedi/config.json\fP
.IP 2. 3
One of the following files: \fBsetup.py\fP, \fB\&.git\fP, \fB\&.hg\fP,
\fBrequirements.txt\fP and \fBMANIFEST.in\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.Project(path, *, environment_path=None, load_unsafe_extensions=False, sys_path=None, added_sys_path=(), smart_sys_path=True)
Projects are a simple way to manage Python folders and define how Jedi does
import resolution. It is mostly used as a parameter to \fBScript\fP\&.
Additionally there are functions to search a whole project.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath\fP \-\- The base path for this project.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenvironment_path\fP \-\- The Python executable path, typically the path
of a virtual environment.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBload_unsafe_extensions\fP \-\- Default False, Loads extensions that are not in the
sys path and in the local directories. With this option enabled,
this is potentially unsafe if you clone a git repository and
analyze it\(aqs code, because those compiled extensions will be
important and therefore have execution privileges.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsys_path\fP \-\- list of str. You can override the sys path if you
want. By default the \fBsys.path.\fP is generated by the
environment (virtualenvs, etc).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBadded_sys_path\fP \-\- list of str. Adds these paths at the end of the
sys path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsmart_sys_path\fP \-\- If this is enabled (default), adds paths from
local directories. Otherwise you will have to rely on your packages
being properly configured on the \fBsys.path\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B classmethod load(path)
Loads a project from a specific path. You should not provide the path
to \fB\&.jedi/project.json\fP, but rather the path to the project folder.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBpath\fP \-\- The path of the directory you want to use as a project.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B save()
Saves the project configuration in the project in \fB\&.jedi/project.json\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B added_sys_path
The sys path that is going to be added at the end of the
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property path
The base path for this project.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property sys_path
The sys path provided to this project. This can be None and in that
case will be auto generated.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property smart_sys_path
If the sys path is going to be calculated in a smart way, where
additional paths are added.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property load_unsafe_extensions
Wheter the project loads unsafe extensions.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B search(string, *, all_scopes=False)
Searches a name in the whole project. If the project is very big,
at some point Jedi will stop searching. However it\(aqs also very much
recommended to not exhaust the generator. Just display the first ten
results to the user.
.sp
There are currently three different search patterns:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo\fP to search for a definition foo in any file or a file called
\fBfoo.py\fP or \fBfoo.pyi\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo.bar\fP to search for the \fBfoo\fP and then an attribute \fBbar\fP
in it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclass foo.bar.Bar\fP or \fBdef foo.bar.baz\fP to search for a specific
API type.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBall_scopes\fP (\fIbool\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:bool>) \-\- Default False; searches not only for
definitions on the top level of a module level, but also in
functions and classes.
.TP
.B Yields
\fBName\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B complete_search(string, **kwargs)
Like \fBScript.search()\fP, but completes that string. An empty string
lists all definitions in a project, so be careful with that.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBall_scopes\fP (\fIbool\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:bool>) \-\- Default False; searches not only for
definitions on the top level of a module level, but also in
functions and classes.
.TP
.B Yields
\fBCompletion\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Completion>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Environments
.sp
Environments are a way to activate different Python versions or Virtualenvs for
static analysis. The Python binary in that environment is going to be executed.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.find_system_environments(*, env_vars=None)
Ignores virtualenvs and returns the Python versions that were installed on
your system. This might return nothing, if you\(aqre running Python e.g. from
a portable version.
.sp
The environments are sorted from latest to oldest Python version.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Yields
\fBEnvironment\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.find_virtualenvs(paths=None, *, safe=True, use_environment_vars=True)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpaths\fP \-\- A list of paths in your file system to be scanned for
Virtualenvs. It will search in these paths and potentially execute the
Python binaries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsafe\fP \-\- Default True. In case this is False, it will allow this
function to execute potential \fIpython\fP environments. An attacker might
be able to drop an executable in a path this function is searching by
default. If the executable has not been installed by root, it will not
be executed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuse_environment_vars\fP \-\- Default True. If True, the VIRTUAL_ENV
variable will be checked if it contains a valid VirtualEnv.
CONDA_PREFIX will be checked to see if it contains a valid conda
environment.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Yields
\fBEnvironment\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.get_system_environment(version, *, env_vars=None)
Return the first Python environment found for a string of the form \(aqX.Y\(aq
where X and Y are the major and minor versions of Python.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Raises
\fBInvalidPythonEnvironment\fP
.TP
.B Returns
\fBEnvironment\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.create_environment(path, *, safe=True, env_vars=None)
Make it possible to manually create an Environment object by specifying a
Virtualenv path or an executable path and optional environment variables.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Raises
\fBInvalidPythonEnvironment\fP
.TP
.B Returns
\fBEnvironment\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.get_default_environment()
Tries to return an active Virtualenv or conda environment.
If there is no VIRTUAL_ENV variable or no CONDA_PREFIX variable set
set it will return the latest Python version installed on the system. This
makes it possible to use as many new Python features as possible when using
autocompletion and other functionality.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Returns
\fBEnvironment\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B exception jedi.InvalidPythonEnvironment
If you see this exception, the Python executable or Virtualenv you have
been trying to use is probably not a correct Python version.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.environment.Environment(executable, env_vars=None)
This class is supposed to be created by internal Jedi architecture. You
should not create it directly. Please use create_environment or the other
functions instead. It is then returned by that function.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_sys_path()
The sys path for this environment. Does not include potential
modifications from e.g. appending to \fBsys.path\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:sys\:.html#\:sys\:.path>\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Returns
list of str
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Helper Functions
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.preload_module(*modules)
Preloading modules tells Jedi to load a module now, instead of lazy parsing
of modules. This can be useful for IDEs, to control which modules to load
on startup.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBmodules\fP \-\- different module names, list of string.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.set_debug_function(func_cb=<function print_to_stdout>, warnings=True, notices=True, speed=True)
Define a callback debug function to get all the debug messages.
.sp
If you don\(aqt specify any arguments, debug messages will be printed to stdout.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBfunc_cb\fP \-\- The callback function for debug messages.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Errors
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B exception jedi.InternalError
This error might happen a subprocess is crashing. The reason for this is
usually broken C code in third party libraries. This is not a very common
thing and it is safe to use Jedi again. However using the same calls might
result in the same error again.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B exception jedi.RefactoringError
Refactorings can fail for various reasons. So if you work with refactorings
like \fBScript.rename()\fP, \fBScript.inline()\fP,
\fBScript.extract_variable()\fP and \fBScript.extract_function()\fP, make
sure to catch these. The descriptions in the errors are usually valuable
for end users.
.sp
A typical \fBRefactoringError\fP would tell the user that inlining is not
possible if no name is under the cursor.
.UNINDENT
.SS Examples
.SS Completions
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
>>> import jedi
>>> code = \(aq\(aq\(aqimport json; json.l\(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = jedi.Script(code, path=\(aqexample.py\(aq)
>>> script
<Script: \(aqexample.py\(aq <SameEnvironment: 3.14.0 in /usr>>
>>> completions = script.complete(1, 19)
>>> completions
[<Completion: load>, <Completion: loads>]
>>> completions[1]
<Completion: loads>
>>> completions[1].complete
\(aqoads\(aq
>>> completions[1].name
\(aqloads\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Type Inference / Goto
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
>>> import jedi
>>> code = \(aq\(aq\(aq\e
\&... def my_func():
\&...     print \(aqcalled\(aq
\&...
\&... alias = my_func
\&... my_list = [1, None, alias]
\&... inception = my_list[2]
\&...
\&... inception()\(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = jedi.Script(code)
>>>
>>> script.goto(8, 1)
[<Name full_name=\(aq__main__.inception\(aq, description=\(aqinception = my_list[2]\(aq>]
>>>
>>> script.infer(8, 1)
[<Name full_name=\(aq__main__.my_func\(aq, description=\(aqdef my_func\(aq>]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS References
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
>>> import jedi
>>> code = \(aq\(aq\(aq\e
\&... x = 3
\&... if 1 == 2:
\&...     x = 4
\&... else:
\&...     del x\(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = jedi.Script(code)
>>> rns = script.get_references(5, 8)
>>> rns
[<Name full_name=\(aq__main__.x\(aq, description=\(aqx = 3\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.x\(aq, description=\(aqx = 4\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.x\(aq, description=\(aqdel x\(aq>]
>>> rns[1].line
3
>>> rns[1].column
4
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Deprecations
.sp
The deprecation process is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
A deprecation is announced in any release.
.IP 2. 3
The next major release removes the deprecated functionality.
.UNINDENT
.SS API Return Classes
.SS Abstract Base Class
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.BaseName(inference_state, name)
Bases: \fBobject\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:object>
.sp
The base class for all definitions, completions and signatures.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property module_path: Path \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:pathlib\:.html#\:pathlib\:.Path> | None \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:constants\:.html#\:None>
Shows the file path of a module. e.g. \fB/usr/lib/python3.14/os.py\fP
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property name
Name of variable/function/class/module.
.sp
For example, for \fBx = None\fP it returns \fB\(aqx\(aq\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
str \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str> or None
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property type
The type of the definition.
.sp
Here is an example of the value of this attribute.  Let\(aqs consider
the following source.  As what is in \fBvariable\fP is unambiguous
to Jedi, \fBjedi.Script.infer()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.infer> should return a list of
definition for \fBsys\fP, \fBf\fP, \fBC\fP and \fBx\fP\&.
.sp
.EX
>>> from jedi import Script
>>> source = \(aq\(aq\(aq
\&... import keyword
\&...
\&... class C:
\&...     pass
\&...
\&... class D:
\&...     pass
\&...
\&... x = D()
\&...
\&... def f():
\&...     pass
\&...
\&... for variable in [keyword, f, C, x]:
\&...     variable\(aq\(aq\(aq
.EE
.sp
.EX
>>> script = Script(source)
>>> defs = script.infer()
.EE
.sp
Before showing what is in \fBdefs\fP, let\(aqs sort it by \fBline\fP
so that it is easy to relate the result to the source code.
.sp
.EX
>>> defs = sorted(defs, key=lambda d: d.line)
>>> print(defs)
[<Name full_name=\(aqkeyword\(aq, description=\(aqmodule keyword\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.C\(aq, description=\(aqclass C\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.D\(aq, description=\(aqinstance D\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.f\(aq, description=\(aqdef f\(aq>]
.EE
.sp
Finally, here is what you can get from \fBtype\fP:
.sp
.EX
>>> defs = [d.type for d in defs]
>>> defs[0]
\(aqmodule\(aq
>>> defs[1]
\(aqclass\(aq
>>> defs[2]
\(aqinstance\(aq
>>> defs[3]
\(aqfunction\(aq
.EE
.sp
Valid values for type are \fBmodule\fP, \fBclass\fP, \fBinstance\fP, \fBfunction\fP,
\fBparam\fP, \fBpath\fP, \fBkeyword\fP, \fBproperty\fP and \fBstatement\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property module_name
The module name, a bit similar to what \fB__name__\fP is in a random
Python module.
.sp
.EX
>>> from jedi import Script
>>> source = \(aqimport json\(aq
>>> script = Script(source, path=\(aqexample.py\(aq)
>>> d = script.infer()[0]
>>> print(d.module_name)
json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B in_builtin_module()
Returns True, if this is a builtin module.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property line
The line where the definition occurs (starting with 1).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property column
The column where the definition occurs (starting with 0).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_definition_start_position()
The (row, column) of the start of the definition range. Rows start with
1, columns start with 0.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
Optional[Tuple[int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>, int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>]]
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_definition_end_position()
The (row, column) of the end of the definition range. Rows start with
1, columns start with 0.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
Optional[Tuple[int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>, int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>]]
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B docstring(raw=False, fast=True)
Return a document string for this completion object.
.sp
Example:
.sp
.EX
>>> from jedi import Script
>>> source = \(aq\(aq\(aq\e
\&... def f(a, b=1):
\&...     \(dqDocument for function f.\(dq
\&... \(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = Script(source, path=\(aqexample.py\(aq)
>>> doc = script.infer(1, len(\(aqdef f\(aq))[0].docstring()
>>> print(doc)
f(a, b=1)

Document for function f.
.EE
.sp
Notice that useful extra information is added to the actual
docstring, e.g. function signatures are prepended to their docstrings.
If you need the actual docstring, use \fBraw=True\fP instead.
.sp
.EX
>>> print(script.infer(1, len(\(aqdef f\(aq))[0].docstring(raw=True))
Document for function f.
.EE
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBfast\fP \-\- Don\(aqt follow imports that are only one level deep like
\fBimport foo\fP, but follow \fBfrom foo import bar\fP\&. This makes
sense for speed reasons. Completing \fIimport a\fP is slow if you use
the \fBfoo.docstring(fast=False)\fP on every object, because it
parses all libraries starting with \fBa\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property description
A description of the \fBName\fP object, which is heavily used
in testing. e.g. for \fBisinstance\fP it returns \fBdef isinstance\fP\&.
.sp
Example:
.sp
.EX
>>> from jedi import Script
>>> source = \(aq\(aq\(aq
\&... def f():
\&...     pass
\&...
\&... class C:
\&...     pass
\&...
\&... variable = f if random.choice([0,1]) else C\(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = Script(source)  # line is maximum by default
>>> defs = script.infer(column=3)
>>> defs = sorted(defs, key=lambda d: d.line)
>>> print(defs)
[<Name full_name=\(aq__main__.f\(aq, description=\(aqdef f\(aq>,
 <Name full_name=\(aq__main__.C\(aq, description=\(aqclass C\(aq>]
>>> str(defs[0].description)
\(aqdef f\(aq
>>> str(defs[1].description)
\(aqclass C\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property full_name
Dot\-separated path of this object.
.sp
It is in the form of \fB<module>[.<submodule>[...]][.<object>]\fP\&.
It is useful when you want to look up Python manual of the
object at hand.
.sp
Example:
.sp
.EX
>>> from jedi import Script
>>> source = \(aq\(aq\(aq
\&... import os
\&... os.path.join\(aq\(aq\(aq
>>> script = Script(source, path=\(aqexample.py\(aq)
>>> print(script.infer(3, len(\(aqos.path.join\(aq))[0].full_name)
os.path.join
.EE
.sp
Notice that it returns \fB\(aqos.path.join\(aq\fP instead of (for example)
\fB\(aqposixpath.join\(aq\fP\&. This is not correct, since the modules name would
be \fB<module \(aqposixpath\(aq ...>\(ga\fP\&. However most users find the latter
more practical.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B is_stub()
Returns True if the current name is defined in a stub file.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B is_side_effect()
Checks if a name is defined as \fBself.foo = 3\fP\&. In case of self, this
function would return False, for foo it would return True.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B goto(*, follow_imports=False, follow_builtin_imports=False, only_stubs=False, prefer_stubs=False)
Like \fBScript.goto()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.goto> (also supports the same params), but does it
for the current name. This is typically useful if you are using
something like \fBScript.get_names()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.get_names>\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfollow_imports\fP \-\- The goto call will follow imports.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfollow_builtin_imports\fP \-\- If follow_imports is True will try to
look up names in builtins (i.e. compiled or extension modules).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBonly_stubs\fP \-\- Only return stubs for this goto call.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprefer_stubs\fP \-\- Prefer stubs to Python objects for this goto call.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B infer(*, only_stubs=False, prefer_stubs=False)
Like \fBScript.infer()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.infer>, it can be useful to understand which type
the current name has.
.sp
Return the actual definitions. I strongly recommend not using it for
your completions, because it might slow down Jedi\&. If you want to
read only a few objects (<=20), it might be useful, especially to get
the original docstrings. The basic problem of this function is that it
follows all results. This means with 1000 completions (e.g.  numpy),
it\(aqs just very, very slow.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBonly_stubs\fP \-\- Only return stubs for this goto call.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprefer_stubs\fP \-\- Prefer stubs to Python objects for this type
inference call.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B parent()
Returns the parent scope of this identifier.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
Name \%<#\:jedi\:.api\:.classes\:.Name>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_line_code(before=0, after=0)
Returns the line of code where this object was defined.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbefore\fP \-\- Add n lines before the current line to the output.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBafter\fP \-\- Add n lines after the current line to the output.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Return str
Returns the line(s) of code or an empty string if it\(aqs a
builtin.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_signatures()
Returns all potential signatures for a function or a class. Multiple
signatures are typical if you use Python stubs with \fB@overload\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBBaseSignature\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B execute()
Uses type inference to \(dqexecute\(dq this identifier and returns the
executed objects.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_type_hint()
Returns type hints like \fBIterable[int]\fP or \fBUnion[int, str]\fP\&.
.sp
This method might be quite slow, especially for functions. The problem
is finding executions for those functions to return something like
\fBCallable[[int, str], str]\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
str \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Name
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.Name(inference_state, definition)
Bases: \fBBaseName\fP
.sp
\fIName\fP objects are returned from many different APIs including
\fBScript.goto()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.goto> or \fBScript.infer()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.infer>\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B defined_names()
List sub\-definitions (e.g., methods in class).
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B is_definition()
Returns True, if defined as a name in a statement, function or class.
Returns False, if it\(aqs a reference to such a definition.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Completion
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.Completion(inference_state, name, stack, like_name_length, is_fuzzy, cached_name=None)
Bases: \fBBaseName\fP
.sp
\fBCompletion\fP objects are returned from \fBScript.complete()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.complete>\&. They
provide additional information about a completion.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property complete
Only works with non\-fuzzy completions. Returns None if fuzzy
completions are used.
.sp
Return the rest of the word, e.g. completing \fBisinstance\fP:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
isinstan# <\-\- Cursor is here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
would return the string \(aqce\(aq. It also adds additional stuff, depending
on your \fBsettings.py\fP\&.
.sp
Assuming the following function definition:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def foo(param=0):
    pass
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
completing \fBfoo(par\fP would give a \fBCompletion\fP which \fBcomplete\fP
would be \fBam=\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property name_with_symbols
Similar to \fBname\fP, but like \fBname\fP returns also the
symbols, for example assuming the following function definition:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def foo(param=0):
    pass
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
completing \fBfoo(\fP would give a \fBCompletion\fP which
\fBname_with_symbols\fP would be \(dqparam=\(dq.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B docstring(raw=False, fast=True)
Documented under \fBBaseName.docstring()\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property type
Documented under \fBBaseName.type()\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_completion_prefix_length()
Returns the length of the prefix being completed.
For example, completing \fBisinstance\fP:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
isinstan# <\-\- Cursor is here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
would return 8, because len(\(aqisinstan\(aq) == 8.
.sp
Assuming the following function definition:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def foo(param=0):
    pass
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
completing \fBfoo(par\fP would return 3.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS BaseSignature
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.BaseSignature(inference_state, signature)
Bases: \fBName\fP
.sp
These signatures are returned by \fBBaseName.get_signatures()\fP
calls.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property params
Returns definitions for all parameters that a signature defines.
This includes stuff like \fB*args\fP and \fB**kwargs\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBParamName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B to_string()
Returns a text representation of the signature. This could for example
look like \fBfoo(bar, baz: int, **kwargs)\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
str \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Signature
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.Signature(inference_state, signature, call_details)
Bases: \fBBaseSignature\fP
.sp
A full signature object is the return value of
\fBScript.get_signatures()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.get_signatures>\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property index
Returns the param index of the current cursor position.
Returns None if the index cannot be found in the curent call.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property bracket_start
Returns a line/column tuple of the bracket that is responsible for the
last function call. The first line is 1 and the first column 0.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>, int \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:int>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS ParamName
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.classes.ParamName(inference_state, definition)
Bases: \fBName\fP
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B infer_default()
Returns default values like the \fB1\fP of \fBdef foo(x=1):\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B infer_annotation(**kwargs)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Parameters
\fBexecute_annotation\fP \-\- Default True; If False, values are not
executed and classes are returned instead of instances.
.TP
.B Return type
list of \fBName\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B to_string()
Returns a simple representation of a param, like
\fBf: Callable[..., Any]\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
str \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:stdtypes\:.html#\:str>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property kind
Returns an enum instance of \fBinspect\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:inspect\:.html#\:module-inspect>\(aqs \fBParameter\fP enum.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B Return type
\fBinspect.Parameter.kind\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:inspect\:.html#\:inspect\:.Parameter\:.kind>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Refactoring
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.refactoring.Refactoring(inference_state, file_to_node_changes, renames=())
Bases: \fBobject\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:object>
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B get_renames() -> Iterable \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:typing\:.html#\:typing\:.Iterable>[Tuple \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:typing\:.html#\:typing\:.Tuple>[Path \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:pathlib\:.html#\:pathlib\:.Path>, Path \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:pathlib\:.html#\:pathlib\:.Path>]]
Files can be renamed in a refactoring.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B apply()
Applies the whole refactoring to the files, which includes renames.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B class jedi.api.errors.SyntaxError(parso_error)
Bases: \fBobject\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:object>
.sp
Syntax errors are generated by \fBScript.get_syntax_errors()\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script\:.get_syntax_errors>\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property line
The line where the error starts (starting with 1).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property column
The column where the error starts (starting with 0).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property until_line
The line where the error ends (starting with 1).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B property until_column
The column where the error ends (starting with 0).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Installation and Configuration
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Most people will want to install Jedi as a submodule/vendored and
not through pip/system wide. The reason for this is that it makes sense that
the plugin that uses Jedi has always access to it. Otherwise Jedi will not
work properly when virtualenvs are activated. So please read the
documentation of your editor/IDE plugin to install Jedi.
.sp
For plugin developers, Jedi works best if it is always available. Vendoring
is a pretty good option for that.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
You can either include Jedi as a submodule in your text editor plugin (like
jedi\-vim \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi-vim> does by default), or you can install it systemwide.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This just installs the Jedi library, not the editor plugins \%<#\:editor-plugins>\&. For information about how to make it work with your
editor, refer to the corresponding documentation.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The normal way
.sp
Most people use Jedi with a editor plugins \%<#\:editor-plugins>\&. Typically
you install Jedi by installing an editor plugin. No necessary steps are needed.
Just take a look at the instructions for the plugin.
.SS With pip
.sp
On any system you can install Jedi directly from the Python package index
using pip:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sudo pip install jedi
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If you want to install the current development version (master branch):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sudo pip install \-e git+https://github.com/davidhalter/jedi.git#egg=jedi
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS System\-wide installation via a package manager
.SS Arch Linux
.sp
You can install Jedi directly from official Arch Linux packages:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
python\-jedi \%<https://\:www\:.archlinux\:.org/\:packages/\:community/\:any/\:python-jedi>
.UNINDENT
.sp
(There is also a packaged version of the vim plugin available:
vim\-jedi at Arch Linux \%<https://\:www\:.archlinux\:.org/\:packages/\:community/\:any/\:vim-jedi>\&.)
.SS Debian
.sp
Debian packages are available in the unstable repository \%<https://\:packages\:.debian\:.org/\:search?\:keywords=python%20jedi>\&.
.SS Others
.sp
We are in the discussion of adding Jedi to the Fedora repositories.
.SS Manual installation from GitHub
.sp
If you prefer not to use an automated package installer, you can clone the source from GitHub and install it manually. To install it, run these commands:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
git clone \-\-recurse\-submodules https://github.com/davidhalter/jedi
cd jedi
sudo python setup.py install
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Inclusion as a submodule
.sp
If you use an editor plugin like jedi\-vim \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi-vim>, you can simply include Jedi as a
git submodule of the plugin directory. Vim plugin managers like Vundle \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:gmarik/\:vundle> or
Pathogen \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:tpope/\:vim-pathogen> make it very easy to keep submodules up to date.
.SS Settings
.sp
This module contains variables with global Jedi settings. To change the
behavior of Jedi, change the variables defined in \fBjedi.settings\fP\&.
.sp
Plugins should expose an interface so that the user can adjust the
configuration.
.sp
Example usage:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from jedi import settings
settings.case_insensitive_completion = True
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Completion output
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.case_insensitive_completion = True
Completions are by default case insensitive.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.add_bracket_after_function = False
Adds an opening bracket after a function for completions.
.UNINDENT
.SS Filesystem cache
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.cache_directory = \(aq/build/.cache/jedi\(aq
The path where the cache is stored.
.sp
On Linux, this defaults to \fB~/.cache/jedi/\fP, on OS X to
\fB~/Library/Caches/Jedi/\fP and on Windows to \fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eJedi\eJedi\e\fP\&.
On Linux, if the environment variable \fB$XDG_CACHE_HOME\fP is set,
\fB$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jedi\fP is used instead of the default one.
.UNINDENT
.SS Parser
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.fast_parser = True
Uses Parso\(aqs diff parser. If it is enabled, this might cause issues, please
read the warning on \fBScript\fP \%<#\:jedi\:.Script>\&. This feature makes it possible to only
parse the parts again that have changed, while reusing the rest of the syntax
tree.
.UNINDENT
.SS Dynamic stuff
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.dynamic_array_additions = True
check for \fIappend\fP, etc. on arrays: [], {}, () as well as list/set calls.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.dynamic_params = True
A dynamic param completion, finds the callees of the function, which define
the params of a function.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.dynamic_params_for_other_modules = True
Do the same for other modules.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.auto_import_modules = [\(aqgi\(aq]
Modules that will not be analyzed but imported, if they contain Python code.
This improves autocompletion for libraries that use \fBsetattr\fP or
\fBglobals()\fP modifications a lot.
.UNINDENT
.SS Caching
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.settings.call_signatures_validity = 3.0
Finding function calls might be slow (0.1\-0.5s). This is not acceptible for
normal writing. Therefore cache it for a short time.
.UNINDENT
.SS Jedi Development
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This documentation is for Jedi developers who want to improve Jedi
itself, but have no idea how Jedi works. If you want to use Jedi for
your IDE, look at the plugin api \%<api\:.html>\&.
It is also important to note that it\(aqs a pretty old version and some things
might not apply anymore.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Introduction
.sp
This page tries to address the fundamental demand for documentation of the
Jedi internals. Understanding a dynamic language is a complex task. Especially
because type inference in Python can be a very recursive task. Therefore Jedi
couldn\(aqt get rid of complexity. I know that \fBsimple is better than complex\fP,
but unfortunately it sometimes requires complex solutions to understand complex
systems.
.sp
In six chapters I\(aqm trying to describe the internals of Jedi:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The Jedi Core
.IP \(bu 2
Core Extensions
.IP \(bu 2
Imports & Modules
.IP \(bu 2
Stubs & Annotations
.IP \(bu 2
Caching & Recursions
.IP \(bu 2
Helper modules
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Testing is not documented here, you\(aqll find that
right here \%<testing\:.html>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The Jedi Core
.sp
The core of Jedi consists of three parts:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Parser
.IP \(bu 2
Python type inference
.IP \(bu 2
API
.UNINDENT
.sp
Most people are probably interested in type inference,
because that\(aqs where all the magic happens. I need to introduce the parser first, because \fBjedi.inference\fP uses it extensively.
.SS Parser
.sp
Jedi used to have its internal parser, however this is now a separate project
and is called parso \%<http://\:parso\:.readthedocs\:.io>\&.
.sp
The parser creates a syntax tree that Jedi analyses and tries to understand.
The grammar that this parser uses is very similar to the official Python
grammar files \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:reference/\:grammar\:.html>\&.
.SS Type inference of python code (inference/__init__.py)
.sp
Type inference of Python code in Jedi is based on three assumptions:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The code uses as least side effects as possible. Jedi understands certain
list/tuple/set modifications, but there\(aqs no guarantee that Jedi detects
everything (list.append in different modules for example).
.IP \(bu 2
No magic is being used:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
metaclasses
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsetattr()\fP / \fB__import__()\fP
.IP \(bu 2
writing to \fBglobals()\fP, \fBlocals()\fP, \fBobject.__dict__\fP
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
The programmer is not a total dick, e.g. like this \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi/\:issues/24> :\-)
.UNINDENT
.sp
The actual algorithm is based on a principle I call lazy type inference.  That
said, the typical entry point for static analysis is calling
\fBinfer_expr_stmt\fP\&. There\(aqs separate logic for autocompletion in the API, the
inference_state is all about inferring an expression.
.sp
TODO this paragraph is not what jedi does anymore, it\(aqs similar, but not the
same.
.sp
Now you need to understand what follows after \fBinfer_expr_stmt\fP\&. Let\(aqs
make an example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import datetime
datetime.date.toda# <\-\- cursor here
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
First of all, this module doesn\(aqt care about completion. It really just cares
about \fBdatetime.date\fP\&. At the end of the procedure \fBinfer_expr_stmt\fP will
return the \fBdate\fP class.
.sp
To \fIvisualize\fP this (simplified):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInferenceState.infer_expr_stmt\fP doesn\(aqt do much, because there\(aqs no assignment.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBContext.infer_node\fP cares for resolving the dotted path
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInferenceState.find_types\fP searches for global definitions of datetime, which
it finds in the definition of an import, by scanning the syntax tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Using the import logic, the datetime module is found.
.IP \(bu 2
Now \fBfind_types\fP is called again by \fBinfer_node\fP to find \fBdate\fP
inside the datetime module.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Now what would happen if we wanted \fBdatetime.date.foo.bar\fP? Two more
calls to \fBfind_types\fP\&. However the second call would be ignored, because the
first one would return nothing (there\(aqs no foo attribute in \fBdate\fP).
.sp
What if the import would contain another \fBExprStmt\fP like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from foo import bar
Date = bar.baz
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Well... You get it. Just another \fBinfer_expr_stmt\fP recursion. It\(aqs really
easy. Python can obviously get way more complicated then this. To understand
tuple assignments, list comprehensions and everything else, a lot more code had
to be written.
.sp
Jedi has been tested very well, so you can just start modifying code. It\(aqs best
to write your own test first for your \(dqnew\(dq feature. Don\(aqt be scared of
breaking stuff. As long as the tests pass, you\(aqre most likely to be fine.
.sp
I need to mention now that lazy type inference is really good because it
only \fIinferes\fP what needs to be \fIinferred\fP\&. All the statements and modules
that are not used are just being ignored.
.SS Inference Values (inference/base_value.py)
.sp
Values are the \(dqvalues\(dq that Python would return. However Values are at the
same time also the \(dqvalues\(dq that a user is currently sitting in.
.sp
A ValueSet is typically used to specify the return of a function or any other
static analysis operation. In jedi there are always multiple returns and not
just one.
.SS Name resolution (inference/finder.py)
.sp
Searching for names with given scope and name. This is very central in Jedi and
Python. The name resolution is quite complicated with descripter,
\fB__getattribute__\fP, \fB__getattr__\fP, \fBglobal\fP, etc.
.sp
If you want to understand name resolution, please read the first few chapters
in \%<http://\:blog\:.ionelmc\:.ro/\:2015/\:02/\:09/\:understanding-python-metaclasses>\&.
.SS Flow checks
.sp
Flow checks are not really mature. There\(aqs only a check for \fBisinstance\fP\&.  It
would check whether a flow has the form of \fBif isinstance(a, type_or_tuple)\fP\&.
Unfortunately every other thing is being ignored (e.g. a == \(aq\(aq would be easy to
check for \-> a is a string). There\(aqs big potential in these checks.
.SS API (api/__init__.py and api/classes.py)
.sp
The API has been designed to be as easy to use as possible. The API
documentation can be found here \%<api\:.html>\&. The API itself contains
little code that needs to be mentioned here. Generally I\(aqm trying to be
conservative with the API.  I\(aqd rather not add new API features if they are not
necessary, because it\(aqs much harder to deprecate stuff than to add it later.
.SS Core Extensions
.sp
Core Extensions is a summary of the following topics:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Iterables & Dynamic Arrays
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
Docstrings
.IP \(bu 2
Refactoring
.UNINDENT
.sp
These topics are very important to understand what Jedi additionally does, but
they could be removed from Jedi and Jedi would still work. But slower and
without some features.
.SS Iterables & Dynamic Arrays (inference/value/iterable.py)
.sp
To understand Python on a deeper level, Jedi needs to understand some of the
dynamic features of Python like lists that are filled after creation:
.sp
Contains all classes and functions to deal with lists, dicts, generators and
iterators in general.
.SS Parameter completion (inference/dynamic_params.py)
.sp
One of the really important features of Jedi is to have an option to
understand code like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def foo(bar):
    bar. # completion here
foo(1)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There\(aqs no doubt wheter bar is an \fBint\fP or not, but if there\(aqs also a call
like \fBfoo(\(aqstr\(aq)\fP, what would happen? Well, we\(aqll just show both. Because
that\(aqs what a human would expect.
.sp
It works as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Jedi sees a param
.IP \(bu 2
search for function calls named \fBfoo\fP
.IP \(bu 2
execute these calls and check the input.
.UNINDENT
.SS Docstrings (inference/docstrings.py)
.sp
Docstrings are another source of information for functions and classes.
\fBjedi.inference.dynamic_params\fP tries to find all executions of functions,
while the docstring parsing is much easier. There are three different types of
docstrings that Jedi understands:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Sphinx \%<http://\:sphinx-doc\:.org/\:markup/\:desc\:.html#\:info-field-lists>
.IP \(bu 2
Epydoc \%<http://\:epydoc\:.sourceforge\:.net/\:manual-fields\:.html>
.IP \(bu 2
Numpydoc \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:numpy/\:numpy/\:blob/\:master/\:doc/\:HOWTO_DOCUMENT\:.rst\:.txt>
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, the sphinx annotation \fB:type foo: str\fP clearly states that the
type of \fBfoo\fP is \fBstr\fP\&.
.sp
As an addition to parameter searching, this module also provides return
annotations.
.SS Refactoring (api/refactoring.py)
.SS Imports & Modules
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Modules \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:modules\:.html#\:modules>
.IP \(bu 2
Builtin Modules
.IP \(bu 2
Imports
.UNINDENT
.SS Compiled Modules (inference/compiled.py)
.SS Imports (inference/imports.py)
.sp
\fBjedi.inference.imports\fP is here to resolve import statements and return
the modules/classes/functions/whatever, which they stand for. However there\(aqs
not any actual importing done. This module is about finding modules in the
filesystem. This can be quite tricky sometimes, because Python imports are not
always that simple.
.sp
This module also supports import autocompletion, which means to complete
statements like \fBfrom datetim\fP (cursor at the end would return \fBdatetime\fP).
.SS Stubs & Annotations (inference/gradual)
.sp
It is unfortunately not well documented how stubs and annotations work in Jedi.
If somebody needs an introduction, please let me know.
.SS Caching & Recursions
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Caching
.IP \(bu 2
Recursions
.UNINDENT
.SS Caching (cache.py)
.sp
This caching is very important for speed and memory optimizations. There\(aqs
nothing really spectacular, just some decorators. The following cache types are
available:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtime_cache\fP can be used to cache something for just a limited time span,
which can be useful if there\(aqs user interaction and the user cannot react
faster than a certain time.
.UNINDENT
.sp
This module is one of the reasons why Jedi is not thread\-safe. As you can see
there are global variables, which are holding the cache information. Some of
these variables are being cleaned after every API usage.
.SS Recursions (recursion.py)
.sp
Recursions are the recipe of Jedi to conquer Python code. However, someone
must stop recursions going mad. Some settings are here to make Jedi stop at
the right time. You can read more about them here\&.
.sp
Next to the internal \fBjedi.inference.cache\fP this module also makes Jedi not
thread\-safe, because \fBexecution_recursion_decorator\fP uses class variables to
count the function calls.
.SS Settings
.sp
Recursion settings are important if you don\(aqt want extremely
recursive python code to go absolutely crazy.
.sp
The default values are based on experiments while completing the Jedi library
itself (inception!). But I don\(aqt think there\(aqs any other Python library that
uses recursion in a similarly extreme way. Completion should also be fast and
therefore the quality might not always be maximal.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.inference.recursion.recursion_limit = 15
Like \fBsys.getrecursionlimit()\fP \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:sys\:.html#\:sys\:.getrecursionlimit>, just for Jedi\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.inference.recursion.total_function_execution_limit = 200
This is a hard limit of how many non\-builtin functions can be executed.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.inference.recursion.per_function_execution_limit = 6
The maximal amount of times a specific function may be executed.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B jedi.inference.recursion.per_function_recursion_limit = 2
A function may not be executed more than this number of times recursively.
.UNINDENT
.SS Helper Modules
.sp
Most other modules are not really central to how Jedi works. They all contain
relevant code, but you if you understand the modules above, you pretty much
understand Jedi.
.SS Jedi Testing
.sp
The test suite depends on \fBpytest\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pip install pytest
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If you want to test only a specific Python version (e.g. Python 3.14), it is as
easy as:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
python3.14 \-m pytest
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Tests are also run automatically on GitHub Actions \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:davidhalter/\:jedi/\:actions>\&.
.sp
You want to add a test for Jedi? Great! We love that. Normally you should
write your tests as Blackbox Tests\&. Most tests would
fit right in there.
.sp
For specific API testing we\(aqre using simple unit tests, with a focus on a
simple and readable testing structure.
.SS Integration Tests (run.py)
.sp
Jedi is mostly being tested by what I would call \(dqintegration tests\(dq. These
tests are testing type inference with the public API. This makes a
lot of sense for Jedi\&. Also, it is hard to write doctests/unittests for
the internal data structures.
.sp
There are different kinds of tests:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
completions / inference \fB#?\fP
.IP \(bu 2
goto: \fB#!\fP
.IP \(bu 2
references: \fB#<\fP
.UNINDENT
.SS How to run tests?
.sp
Jedi uses pytest \%<http://\:pytest\:.org> to run unit and integration tests.  To run tests,
simply run \fBpytest\fP\&.
.sp
Most integration test cases are located in the \fBtest/completion\fP directory
and each test case starts with one of these comments:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#?\fP (completions / inference)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#!\fP (goto)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#<\fP (references)
.UNINDENT
.sp
There is also support for third party libraries. In a normal test run they are
not being executed, you have to provide a \fB\-\-thirdparty\fP option.
.sp
In addition to pytest\(aqs \fB\-k\fP and \fB\-m\fP options, you can use the
\fB\-T\fP (\fB\-\-test\-files\(ga) option to specify which test cases should run.
It takes the format of \(ga\(gaFILE_NAME[:LINE[,LINE[,...]]]\fP where
\fBFILE_NAME\fP is a file in \fBtest/completion\fP and \fBLINE\fP is a line
number of the test comment.  Here are some examples:
.sp
Run tests only in \fBcompletion/basic.py\fP and \fBcompletion/imports.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pytest test/test_integration.py \-T basic.py \-T imports.py
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Run test at line 4, 6, and 8 in \fBcompletion/basic.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pytest test/test_integration.py \-T basic.py:4,6,8
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
See \fBpytest \-\-help\fP for more information.
.sp
If you want to debug a test, just use the \fB\-\-pdb\fP option.
.SS Alternate Test Runner
.sp
If you don\(aqt like the output of \fBpytest\fP, there\(aqs an alternate test runner
that you can start by running \fB\&./run.py\fP\&. The above example could be run by:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&./run.py basic 4 6 8 50\-80
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The advantage of this runner is simplicity and more customized error reports.
.SS Auto\-Completion Tests
.sp
Uses a comment to specify a test on the next line. The comment defines the
expected completions. The comment always begins with \fI#?\fP\&. The last row
symbolizes the cursor. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#? [\(aqupper\(aq]
a = \(aqfoo\(aq; a.upp
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Inference Tests
.sp
Inference tests look very simliar. The difference is that inference tests don\(aqt
use brackets:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#? int()
ab = 3; ab
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Goto Tests
.sp
Goto Tests look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
abc = 1
#! [\(aqabc=1\(aq]
abc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Additionally it is possible to specify the column by adding a number, which
describes the position of the test (otherwise it\(aqs just the end of line):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#! 2 [\(aqabc=1\(aq]
abc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Reference Tests
.sp
Tests look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
abc = 1
#< (1,0), (3,0)
abc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Refactoring Tests (refactor.py)
.sp
Refactoring tests work a little bit similar to integration tests. But the idea
is here to compare two versions of code. If you want to add a new test case,
just look at the existing ones in the \fBtest/refactor\fP folder and copy them.
.SS History & Acknowledgements
.SS Acknowledgements
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Dave Halter for creating and maintaining Jedi & Parso.
.IP \(bu 2
Takafumi Arakaki (@tkf) for creating a solid test environment and a lot of
other things.
.IP \(bu 2
Danilo Bargen (@dbrgn) for general housekeeping and being a good friend :).
.IP \(bu 2
Guido van Rossum (@gvanrossum) for creating the parser generator pgen2
(originally used in lib2to3).
.IP \(bu 2
Thanks to all the contributors\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS A Little Bit of History
.sp
Written by Dave.
.sp
The Star Wars Jedi are awesome. My Jedi software tries to imitate a little bit
of the precognition the Jedi have. There\(aqs even an awesome scene \%<https://\:youtu\:.be/\:yHRJLIf7wMU> of Monty Python Jedis :\-).
.sp
But actually the name has not much to do with Star Wars. It\(aqs part of my
second name Jedidjah.
.sp
I actually started Jedi back in 2012, because there were no good solutions
available for VIM. Most auto\-completion solutions just did not work well. The
only good solution was PyCharm. But I liked my good old VIM very much. There
was also a solution called Rope that did not work at all for me. So I decided
to write my own version of a completion engine.
.sp
The first idea was to execute non\-dangerous code. But I soon realized, that
this would not work. So I started to build a static analysis tool.
The biggest problem that I had at the time was that I did not know a thing
about parsers. I did not even know the word static analysis. It turns
out they are the foundation of a good static analysis tool. I of course did not
know that and tried to write my own poor version of a parser that I ended up
throwing away two years later.
.sp
Because of my lack of knowledge, everything after 2012 and before 2020 was
basically refactoring. I rewrote the core parts of Jedi probably like 5\-10
times. The last big rewrite (that I did twice) was the inclusion of
gradual typing and stubs.
.sp
I learned during that time that it is crucial to have a good understanding of
your problem. Otherwise you just end up doing it again. I only wrote features
in the beginning and in the end. Everything else was bugfixing and refactoring.
However now I am really happy with the result. It works well, bugfixes can be
quick and is pretty much feature complete.

.sp
.ce
----

.ce 0
.sp
.sp
I will leave you with a small anecdote that happened in 2012, if I remember
correctly. After I explained Guido van Rossum, how some parts of my
auto\-completion work, he said:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fI\(dqOh, that worries me...\(dq\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Now that it is finished, I hope he likes it :\-).
